 "analysis": {
  "headline": "Triage scores rank ClamAV DoS above NGINX RCE \u2014 metadata, not risk, sets the order",
  "summary": [
   "Scope reconciles at the release level: 16 CVEs scoped against 16 vendor patches, and the vendor flagged nothing notable. Below that, the machine fields do not reconcile. Severity buckets are empty (0 critical, 0 important, 0 moderate, 0 low) while the scored rows carry High/7.5; family counts sum to 21 across four families against a 16-CVE scope; and the EPSS aggregate reports 6 at \u226510% and 6 at \u226550% as the same set, which the per-row values contradict (14 of 16 rows sit above 10%). KEV, exploited, disclosed, more_likely and remote_unauth are all zero, so no exploitation signal exists anywhere in this release \u2014 the urgency ordering here is built from scoring inputs, not observed activity.",
   "The triage order is inverted against consequence. Ten ClamAV engine bugs in Secure Endpoint occupy the top of the ranking on CVSS 7.5 plus an automatable flag, with EPSS in the 37\u201350% band and no exploitation flags; they are availability defects in a scanner, plus two scan-time bypasses. The five Intersight CVEs and the OpenJDK/Java4Shell CVE \u2014 EPSS 0.97 to 0.99, the highest in the release \u2014 rank 23\u201331 and carry no CVSS and no severity at all, so they are excluded from every severity bucket by construction. Treat the ranking as a completeness measure for metadata, not a risk ranking."
